---
// Specific's story, from Iman Radjavi's answers (2026-09-26).
import { NEW_TAB_ATTRS } from "blume/core/new-tab.ts";
import Quote from "../_customers/Quote.astro";
import StoryPage from "../_customers/StoryPage.astro";
import { storyById } from "../_customers/customers.ts";
---

<StoryPage story={storyById("specific")}>
  <p>
    <a href="https://specific.dev" {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>Specific</a> (YC F25) is
    the cloud platform built for coding agents. Your agent already writes your
    code; Specific lets it build the infrastructure to run it too. The agent
    defines the resources an app needs in a config file, spins up the whole
    stack locally with one command, tests against isolated production data in
    preview environments, and ships with a single command or through GitHub.
    When something breaks, it queries logs and metrics, finds the root cause,
    and ships the fix.
  </p>
  <p>
    The team runs as much as it can on Specific: the landing page, the
    dashboard, the docs, and Hermes, its internal agent. That way it hits the
    same problems its customers do, first.
  </p>

  <h2>The challenge</h2>
  <p>
    Specific wanted two things from its docs: a beautiful site out of the box,
    and preview environments, so the team could check what its agents changed
    before shipping. The docs site isn't Specific's primary surface, since
    agents mostly read the docs that ship inside its CLI, so anything beyond
    that was a bonus.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mintlify did both. But it was a closed, hosted product, so the docs were
    the one part of Specific not running on Specific, and at $500 a month it
    cost a lot for what the team needed.
  </p>

  <Quote
    avatar="/customers/iman-radjavi.webp"
    name="Iman Radjavi"
    role="Co-founder, Specific"
  >
    Our docs were the one part of Specific not running on Specific. Blume fixed
    that in an afternoon.
  </Quote>

  <h2>The move</h2>
  <p>
    When Blume came out, the choice was easy: open source and self-hostable
    meant Specific could run it on Specific. Iman handed the migration to
    Claude, and a couple of hours later, on July 14, 2026, the switch was made.
  </p>
  <p>
    The whole deployment is one <code>specific.hcl</code> of about 30 lines: a
    build step, a service running the{" "}
    <a href="/docs/deployment#server-rendering">Node server</a> Blume builds, a
    public endpoint with a health check, and one secret for the assistant. Every branch
    gets a preview environment out of the box, which covered the other half of
    what the team wanted.
  </p>

  <h2>The assistant, out of the box</h2>
  <p>
    Since agents mostly read the docs in Specific's CLI, what the team values
    in Blume isn't any single AI feature but how cheap they are to turn on. The{" "}
    <a href="/docs/discoverability/mcp">MCP server</a> at <code>/mcp</code> is
    on because it was one line of config.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="/docs/configuration/assistant">assistant</a> is the one that paid
    off. Blume ships an assistant endpoint grounded in the docs, so any page can
    call it. After seeing Gustavo's "infinite FAQ" demo on X, Iman{" "}
    <a
      href="https://x.com/imanradjavi/status/2101674436210434246"
      {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>built one for specific.dev</a
    > the same day: the FAQ on Specific's landing page ends with an empty row where
    a visitor types their own question and gets an answer from the docs, two to
    five sentences long, citing at most two pages.
  </p>
  <p>
    It needed two small options Blume didn't have yet:{" "}
    <a href="/docs/configuration/assistant#cross-origin-callers">CORS</a>, for
    a caller on another origin, and a{" "}
    <a href="/docs/configuration/assistant#reasoning">reasoning level</a>. Iman
    sent two pull requests, and both were merged and released the same day.
    Iman calls that turnaround the real feature.
  </p>

  <Quote
    avatar="/customers/iman-radjavi.webp"
    name="Iman Radjavi"
    role="Co-founder, Specific"
  >
    When a feature was missing, I could contribute it myself and see it land in
    the next release.
  </Quote>

  <h2>The results</h2>
  <p>
    On the first Blume deploy, before any tuning, Lighthouse performance went
    from 87 to 100, and every score is now 96 or above. Both runs were on the
    same URL on July 14, 2026 (<a
      href="https://x.com/imanradjavi/status/2077040598721175972"
      {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>before and after</a
    >).
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Lighthouse</th>
        <th scope="col">Mintlify</th>
        <th scope="col">Blume</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Performance</th><td>87</td><td>100</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Accessibility</th><td>95</td><td>98</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Best Practices</th><td>96</td><td>96</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">SEO</th><td>100</td><td>100</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Docs hosting went from $500 a month to a couple dollars of compute, and the
    migration took a couple of hours of agent time. Iman{" "}
    <a
      href="https://www.linkedin.com/posts/iman-radjavi_we-saved-500-a-month-moving-our-docs-from-share-7503076122100785152-HYWD/"
      {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>announced the move on LinkedIn</a
    >, and the new docs are live at{" "}
    <a href="https://docs.specific.dev" {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>docs.specific.dev</a
    >.
  </p>
</StoryPage>
